>> title: Seven-Minute Eggs
>> category: Eggs
>> tags: eggs, boiled, jammy, topping, quick
>> counters: The Bowl Shop
>> aka: jammy eggs, soft-boiled eggs, seven minute egg, 7-minute egg, medium-boiled eggs, soft boiled egg, bowl egg
>> pairs-with: mujaddara, harissa
>> servings: 6
>> time: 20 min
>> slack: unforgiving — thirty seconds either side is a running yolk or a chalky ring, and there is no way back from either
>> step.1: boil 7 min from fridge-cold
>> step.2: ice bath 5 min
>> step.3: peel under the tap
>> step.4: halve and salt at the table

Lower @eggs{6%large}(300 g; straight from the fridge) into hard-boiling @water{2%qt}(1.9 L) on a spoon and ~boil{7%min}, keeping the water at a rolling boil the whole time. Into boiling water rather than starting cold: a cold start gives you no clock at all, because the time depends on your pan, your burner and how much water is in it. Seven minutes flat gives a fully set white and a yolk that is thick and bright orange at the middle. This is not `ajitama`, which runs six and a half so the centre can survive a night in soy — these get eaten within the hour and want the extra thirty seconds.

Move @&(~1)boiled eggs{} straight into @ice water{1%qt}(950 mL) and ~cool{5%min}. The ice stops the carry-over, which is real: an egg left in its own heat for two more minutes on the counter is a seven-and-a-half-minute egg.

Crack @&(~1)chilled eggs{} all over on the counter and peel them under a running cold tap, starting at the fat end where the air pocket is. The water gets under the membrane and lifts it; peeling dry is how you end up with a cratered egg.

Halve @&(~1)peeled eggs{} with a wet knife and season the cut faces with @flaky salt{1/2%tsp} and @black pepper{1/4%tsp}. Wipe the blade between eggs or the yolk drags. They keep three days unpeeled in the fridge and about a day peeled.
